  "OverallSummary": "This case involves a Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) Care Obligation violation by Mary Christine Beslagic, who was associated with Edward Jones until her discharge in 2022. In March 2022, Beslagic recommended that a married couple invest $220,000 from a home equity loan in mutual funds primarily designed for long-term growth, despite knowing the customers needed these funds for near-term purposes (purchasing a house for a family member and renovating their own home). The recommended mutual funds carried risk of short-term capital loss, which materialized when the investments declined in value shortly after purchase. This forced the customers to sell a portion at a loss and take out margin loans totaling approximately $25,000 to meet their liquidity needs. The firm later compensated the customers $54,216 for their losses. FINRA determined that Beslagic willfully violated Rule 15l-1(a)(1) under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (Reg BI) and FINRA Rule 2010 because she did not have a reasonable basis to believe her recommended investment strategy was in the customers' best interest. The analytical steps in determining this violation were: (1) establishing that the broker knew about the customers' near-term liquidity needs, (2) determining that the recommended investments were inappropriate given those needs due to risk of short-term capital loss, and (3) concluding that the broker failed to consider the customers' investment profile when making recommendations. As a result, Beslagic received a two-month suspension and a $5,000 fine."
